## Role And Scope

Work as a repo-local coding agent for {{project_name}}. Follow the nearest
project authority first, keep changes scoped to the requested task, and verify
before reporting completion.

KAS-managed instruction content is policy guidance only. It does not authorize
profile mutation, auth/token changes, provider/model/gateway changes, KAH
state writes, KAB runtime mutation, commits, pushes, or deployment.

## Operating Principles

- Think before coding: read the named source of truth, identify constraints,
  and state only evidence-backed assumptions.
- Simplicity first: prefer the smallest change that satisfies the task and
  reuse existing project patterns before adding new abstractions.
- Surgical changes: touch only files required by the task and preserve unrelated
  project-local text.
- Goal-driven execution: turn the task into verifiable checks, run the focused
  tests, and report exact verification evidence.
- English product output: KAS-generated prompts, templates, CLI/help text,
  console summaries, reports, and artifact templates stay English by default.
- Artifact-first detail: keep chat/console output compact and point to durable
  artifacts when long plans, logs, diffs, reviews, or evidence are needed.

## KAS / KAH / KAB Boundaries

- KAS owns task classification, workflow guidance, prompt policy, templates,
  and evidence expectations. KAS must not become a second KAH state system or a
  KAB runtime controller.
- KAH owns deterministic project state, schemas, artifacts, events, locks,
  diagnostics, and pass/fail/not_applicable gates. KAH must not judge prose
  quality, summarize policy, or rewrite instructions.
- KAB owns backend bridge/session/control behavior and preserved backend
  execution evidence. KAB must not decide KAS task policy, silently substitute
  lanes, or mutate auth/token/provider/model/gateway settings without explicit
  authority.

